dict(sec=25, img=None,
stem="A 74-year-old man with severe aortic stenosis (mean gradient 46 mm Hg, valve area 0.8 cm²) denies any symptoms and walks daily without limitation. Echocardiography shows an ejection fraction of 42%, down from 60% two years ago.{FIG}Which of the following is the most appropriate management?",
choices=["Proceed to aortic valve replacement now","Balloon aortic valvuloplasty as definitive therapy","Repeat echocardiography in 12 months","Start a beta-blocker and lisinopril, then observe","Treadmill stress test to provoke symptoms first"], key=0,
exp="Indications for aortic valve replacement in aortic stenosis: <b>severe aortic stenosis (AS) with any symptom</b> (angina, syncope, dyspnea, reduced exercise tolerance); <b>severe AS with an ejection fraction under 50%</b> even without symptoms; and severe AS when another cardiac operation (such as coronary artery bypass grafting (CABG)) is being done. A falling ejection fraction (EF) means the ventricle is decompensating, so he needs replacement now. No medicine slows AS; balloon valvuloplasty is only a temporary bridge.<br><br><b>Educational objective:</b> Severe aortic stenosis with an ejection fraction under 50% is an indication for valve replacement even without symptoms.",
wrong={1:"Balloon aortic valvuloplasty gives only temporary relief and restenosis is rapid; it is a bridge, not definitive therapy.",
2:"Waiting allows irreversible ventricular damage once the EF has fallen.",
3:"No medical therapy alters the course of aortic stenosis.",
4:"Stress testing is for apparently asymptomatic patients with a normal EF; his reduced EF is already an indication."},
eli5="The tight valve is wearing out his heart muscle, even though he feels fine. Replace the valve before the muscle gets permanently weak."),

dict(sec=25, img=None,
stem="A 67-year-old man with symptomatic severe aortic stenosis is scheduled for surgical aortic valve replacement. He has hypertension and hyperlipidemia and is a former smoker. He has no angina.{FIG}Which of the following preoperative studies is mandatory?",
choices=["Bone scan","Coronary angiography","Pulmonary angiography","Renal biopsy","Upper endoscopy"], key=1,
exp="<b>Coronary catheterization is mandatory before surgical aortic valve replacement (AVR)</b> in adults: about <b>50% of AVR patients have coronary disease</b> that needs bypass grafting at the same operation (CT coronary angiography can screen a young, low-risk patient). The rest of the work-up: echo for the valve and left ventricle, carotid Dopplers in those at risk, pulmonary function in heavy smokers, a Society of Thoracic Surgeons (STS) risk score, and a heart-team review for high-risk patients.<br><br><b>Educational objective:</b> Coronary angiography is required before surgical aortic valve replacement, because about half of patients need concomitant bypass.",
wrong={0:"A bone scan has no role.",
2:"Pulmonary angiography looks for embolism, not coronary disease.",
3:"Renal biopsy has no role.",
4:"Endoscopy is not part of routine valve work-up."},
eli5="Half the people needing a new aortic valve also have blocked heart arteries. Check them first so the surgeon can bypass them in the same operation."),

dict(sec=25, img=None,
stem="An 84-year-old woman with symptomatic severe calcific aortic stenosis has chronic kidney disease, frailty and severe chronic obstructive pulmonary disease; her Society of Thoracic Surgeons (STS) predicted surgical mortality is 9%. CT shows a heavily calcified aortic annulus and femoral arteries of adequate size.{FIG}Which of the following is the most appropriate treatment?",
choices=["Balloon aortic valvuloplasty alone","Medical therapy with diuretics only","Surgical replacement with a mechanical valve","Surgical replacement with a tissue valve","Transfemoral catheter valve replacement"], key=4,
exp="<b>Transfemoral transcatheter aortic valve replacement (TAVR)</b> suits elderly, frail, high-surgical-risk patients with isolated aortic stenosis, a <b>calcified annulus</b> to anchor the valve, and adequate femoral access. A balloon-mounted (or self-expanding) stented tissue valve is deployed inside the native valve during rapid pacing — \"kind of like having a percutaneous coronary intervention (PCI)\" — with about 2.5% mortality even in high-risk patients and a fast recovery (Mick Jagger was back on tour in about 2 weeks). It is now extending to lower-risk patients; Dr. Qazi calls it first-line for most aortic stenosis (AS).<br><br><b>Educational objective:</b> Transfemoral TAVR is preferred for elderly, high-risk patients with calcific aortic stenosis and suitable access.",
wrong={0:"Valvuloplasty alone gives only temporary relief.",
1:"Symptomatic severe AS treated medically carries about 50% mortality in 2 years.",
2:"Open surgery carries high risk for her, and a mechanical valve needs lifelong warfarin.",
3:"Her surgical risk is high; TAVR offers similar benefit with lower risk."},
eli5="She's too frail for open-heart surgery, so a new valve goes in through a catheter from the leg artery."),

dict(sec=25, img=None,
stem="A 40-year-old man with Marfan syndrome has severe pure aortic regurgitation and an aortic root of 5.1 cm. He asks whether he can have a catheter-based valve to avoid open surgery.{FIG}Which of the following best explains why he needs open surgery rather than a catheter valve?",
choices=["Catheter valves are made only in small sizes","Marfan tissue reacts to the nitinol frame","Catheter valves are mechanical and need warfarin","The uncalcified annulus cannot anchor the valve","Transfemoral access is impossible in Marfan syndrome"], key=3,
exp="A transcatheter aortic valve replacement (TAVR) valve <b>anchors by compressing a calcified annulus</b> and native leaflets. <b>Pure aortic regurgitation</b>, especially with a <b>dilated root</b>, usually has too little calcium, so the valve would migrate or embolize, and TAVR cannot replace the root. He needs <b>surgical valve and root replacement</b> (surgical aortic valve replacement (SAVR), or a valve-sparing root operation). SAVR is also preferred in younger patients wanting a mechanical valve, when the ascending aorta needs replacing, with coronary artery bypass grafting (CABG) or another valve operation, with a bicuspid aortopathy, or with poor femoral access.<br><br><b>Educational objective:</b> TAVR is contraindicated in pure aortic regurgitation because an uncalcified annulus cannot anchor the valve; these patients need surgery.",
wrong={0:"Size limits exist, but the main problem is the absence of annular calcium.",
1:"There is no specific reaction of Marfan tissue to the frame.",
2:"All TAVR valves are tissue valves, not mechanical.",
4:"Femoral access is usually possible; anchoring is the problem."},
eli5="A catheter valve holds on by wedging into a stiff, calcium-crusted ring. A leaky valve with a stretched, smooth root gives it nothing to grip."),

dict(sec=25, img=None,
stem="A 58-year-old man with symptomatic severe aortic stenosis of a tricuspid valve has a low surgical risk. He has read that transcatheter aortic valve replacement (TAVR) has a faster recovery and asks why his surgeon is recommending open surgical replacement.{FIG}Which of the following is the best reason to favor surgical replacement in this patient?",
choices=["Durability of TAVR valves beyond about 10 years is uncertain","Open surgery has lower early mortality than TAVR in low-risk patients","Surgical tissue valves avoid the need for aspirin afterward","TAVR is approved only for patients older than 80 years","A failed TAVR valve cannot be treated again"], key=0,
exp="Current-generation TAVR valves track like surgical tissue valves with data out to about 9 years, but <b>what happens after year 10 is unknown</b> (one slide's \"50% structural degeneration at 8 years\" most likely describes early-generation valves). A 58-year-old may live another 25–30 years, so <b>durability</b> — and the option of a mechanical valve, which \"works forever\" — is why younger patients still lean toward <b>surgical aortic valve replacement (SAVR)</b>. Low-risk SAVR mortality is under 1%, with about 5 days in hospital and 4–6 weeks for the sternum to heal.<br><br><b>Educational objective:</b> Younger patients often choose SAVR because long-term durability of TAVR valves beyond about 10 years is unknown.",
wrong={1:"In the low-risk trials, early mortality after TAVR was similar to or lower than after surgery.",
2:"Surgical tissue valves are followed by aspirin; mechanical valves need warfarin.",
3:"TAVR is now used across risk groups, including low-risk patients in their 60s and 70s.",
4:"A failing TAVR valve can be treated with a valve-in-valve procedure or surgery, although the options narrow."},
eli5="Catheter valves are fairly new, so nobody knows yet how they hold up after 10 years. A younger man may need his valve to last much longer."),

dict(sec=25, img='F_bileaflet', cap="Photograph of a prosthetic heart valve",
stem="A 46-year-old man has the prosthetic valve shown placed in the aortic position. Six months later he asks to switch to apixaban because of the dietary restrictions and blood tests of his current anticoagulant. He is also scheduled for a screening colonoscopy with possible polypectomy.{FIG}Which of the following is the most appropriate advice?",
choices=["He may stop anticoagulation entirely after 6 months","Stay on warfarin; hold it briefly, no bridging","Switch to apixaban, continued through the procedure","Switch to aspirin alone","Switch to dabigatran, held for 2 days before the procedure"], key=1,
exp="A <b>bileaflet mechanical valve</b> (pyrolytic carbon leaflets in a ring with a cloth sewing cuff) \"works forever\" but needs <b>lifelong warfarin</b> — <b>direct oral anticoagulants are not safe</b> with mechanical valves (dabigatran caused more thrombosis and bleeding in trials). For a procedure such as polypectomy, a <b>bileaflet aortic valve with no other thrombotic risk factors</b> needs only a <b>brief interruption of warfarin without heparin bridging</b>; bridging is reserved for mechanical <b>mitral</b> valves, older-generation valves, or added risk factors (atrial fibrillation, prior thromboembolism, a hypercoagulable state, left ventricular dysfunction). The aortic international normalized ratio (INR) target is about 2.5 (higher in the mitral position). Bioprosthetic valves need only aspirin long term.<br><br><b>Educational objective:</b> Mechanical valves need lifelong warfarin (never a direct oral anticoagulant (DOAC)); for a bileaflet aortic valve without risk factors, warfarin is held briefly for procedures without heparin bridging.",
wrong={0:"Mechanical valves require lifelong anticoagulation; stopping risks valve thrombosis and stroke.",
2:"Apixaban is not approved or safe for mechanical valves.",
3:"Aspirin alone is inadequate for a mechanical valve.",
4:"Dabigatran increased thrombotic and bleeding events with mechanical valves."},
eli5="Metal valves make clots easily and need warfarin forever. The newer blood thinners don't work for them. For a small procedure, warfarin is simply paused for a few days."),

